+Use dynamic_vars for single-call substitutions; campaign recipient variables are a different contract. There is no fallback to an agent-level phone_number.
+
+The sender rule is off by default. With no sender, the account normally uses the oldest eligible assigned number, ordered by creation time and then row ID. With the rule on, two or more settled numbers on the same account and agent require an explicit sender. Disabled numbers and numbers on an unsupported carrier still count as settled attachments. Pending imports do not. With just one settled number, the call can use it only if eligible. With none or one ineligible number, expect `agent_missing_phone_number`. An explicit sender still passes the ownership and eligibility checks.
+
+Contact-hours refusals use error.code outside_hours or needs_timezone (409), or contact_policy_unavailable (503). Details include outcome:not_sent, sent:false and charged:false. Advisory messages can instead start “Outside calling hours” or “Weekend where they are” and contain the next local window. single_contact_policy is an internal policy-check name, not an HTTP error code. refused_before_dispatch is a dispatch outcome marker, not an HTTP status or a replacement error.code. Unknown outcome is not proof that nothing was sent.
+
+Optional `request_id` is a UUID saved before one deliberate call intent. Save the exact original request, including the presence or absence of `prompt_validation`. Adding strict mode or changing the sender under the same UUID returns `409 request_id_conflict`. A saved refusal has `state:"rejected"` and `safe_to_retry:false`. Reconcile it before making a corrected, deliberate new request. A same-ID accepted replay returns HTTP 200 with the same call_id and replayed:true; it does not return to/from. Pending/unknown repeats return 409 and never dispatch again. A 201 with request_status:unconfirmed still preserves the known call ID and safe_to_retry:false. Read the saved UUID through [outbound request status](/api-reference/calls/get-outbound-request).

